{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Measured: the liveness rule is inconsistent across relations, and BOTH of P6's remedies are refused\n\nFiling this ticket produced the comparison for free. Against closed tickets, on the same\nstore, in the same minute:\n\n| Attempted link | Endpoint that is closed | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `0461 --depends_on--> 2041` | target | **REFUSED** — \"target ticket is closed\" |\n| `2041 --blocks--> 0461` | source | **REFUSED** — \"source ticket is closed. Reopen it first with: `ticket transition 2041 closed open`\" |\n| `1544 --relates_to--> 868c` | target | ok |\n| `1544 --relates_to--> 266e` | target | ok |\n| `1544 --discovered_from--> 0461` | — (open) | ok |\n\nTwo things this settles.\n\n**1. The link layer does not apply one liveness rule.** `relates_to` accepts a closed target\nwithout complaint; `depends_on` refuses one. Nothing about the two relations justifies the\nsplit — if anything the case for allowing it is stronger for `depends_on`, which records a\nreal ordering between two pieces of work, than for `relates_to`, which records only that\nsomeone found them relevant to each other. So the defect is not \"`depends_on` forgot to allow\nclosed targets\"; it is that endpoint liveness is decided relation by relation with no stated\nrule, so each relation's behaviour is whatever its own code path happened to do. `868c` is the\nsame thing on `supersedes`/closed-source. That is 3 relations with 3 answers and no table.\n\n**2. `P6`'s constructive remedies are both unavailable, not just one.** The criterion offers\nthree: add `depends_on`, ensure the cited ticket `blocks` this one, or remove the citation.\nThe first two are the same edge seen from either end, and both are refused the moment the\nupstream ticket merges. Only \"remove the citation\" survives — discarding accurate provenance\nto satisfy a tool. So a plan that builds on MERGED work can never ground its citation to the\nticket that delivered that work, which is the case where the citation is most certainly\ncorrect.\n\nThe `blocks` refusal also suggests its own remedy: \"Reopen it first.\" Reopening a merged,\nverified, closed ticket in order to record that later work depends on it would corrupt the\ntracker's record of what is done to work around a link-layer rule.\n\n## Not a blocker for 0461\n\n`P6` is advisory (`decision: advisory`, `severity: minor`, `impact: 0.4`), so 0461's review is\nnot held by this. The cost is narrower and worth stating precisely: the citation to the ticket\nthat delivered the code 0461 builds on is \"grounded as normal (fails closed)\" instead of as a\nverified prerequisite. The review still runs; it just cannot use the strongest evidence\navailable to it.\n\n## Unverified\n\nWhether `duplicates` and `caused_by` enforce liveness, and on which endpoint, has not been\nmeasured. The fix's table needs all 6 relations, not the 4 observed here."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793633704033365031,"uuid":"ed5af5c4-2762-4b03-a468-16ed62a2cdda"}